WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.364
One visit to our lab app, hello-devops, took 835 milliseconds.

2
00:00:07.364 --> 00:00:10.223
Its log line says status 200, success.

3
00:00:10.223 --> 00:00:13.984
The dashboard says the error rate is zero percent.

4
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.802
Everything says fine.

5
00:00:15.802 --> 00:00:18.776
So where did those 835 milliseconds go?

6
00:00:18.776 --> 00:00:20.225
Hold that question.

7
00:00:20.225 --> 00:00:22.589
We'll answer it with a picture.

8
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.484
Let's leave software for a minute.

9
00:00:25.484 --> 00:00:28.058
You order a birthday present for a friend.

10
00:00:28.058 --> 00:00:29.713
The shop promises two days.

11
00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:31.305
It takes five.

12
00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:32.611
You call around.

13
00:00:32.611 --> 00:00:35.483
Every depot on the way kept its own logbook.

14
00:00:35.483 --> 00:00:40.248
And every logbook says the same thing: a parcel passed through, all fine.

15
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:45.359
Three honest logbooks, and nobody can say where your parcel sat for three days.

16
00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:48.599
Then you notice the tracking number on the label.

17
00:00:48.599 --> 00:00:52.684
Each depot scanned it as the parcel came in, and again as it left.

18
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.531
Put the scans in order, on one timeline.

19
00:00:55.733 --> 00:00:58.199
In at depot A, out of depot A.

20
00:00:58.199 --> 00:00:59.350
In at depot B.

21
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:01.076
And out of depot B...

22
00:01:01.076 --> 00:01:02.309
two days later.

23
00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:04.135
There it is.

24
00:01:04.135 --> 00:01:06.672
Your parcel sat on a shelf in depot B.

25
00:01:06.672 --> 00:01:12.015
One number, copied along the whole road, turned a pile of logbooks into a story.

26
00:01:12.933 --> 00:01:14.935
Software has the same trouble.

27
00:01:14.935 --> 00:01:17.537
Picture a checkout on a shop's website.

28
00:01:17.537 --> 00:01:23.343
The request enters an API service, a program that answers requests from other programs.

29
00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:27.367
The API calls a fraud check, which calls a card partner.

30
00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:29.674
The API also writes to a database.

31
00:01:29.674 --> 00:01:32.931
Each one is a separate service with its own job.

32
00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:35.425
The customer waited three seconds.

33
00:01:35.425 --> 00:01:36.773
Which hop took them?

34
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.213
Metrics give us numbers per service: the fraud check's 95th percentile went up.

35
00:01:42.213 --> 00:01:50.197
Logs give us lines per service, each with its own timestamp, and we have to guess which lines belong to this one request.

36
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.205
They are the depots' logbooks.

37
00:01:52.205 --> 00:01:53.408
Honest, and no help.

38
00:01:53.408 --> 00:01:57.439
Wouldn't it be nice to put a tracking number on the request itself?

39
00:01:58.167 --> 00:01:59.741
That's what a trace is.

40
00:01:59.741 --> 00:02:06.241
Every service records each step of the request as a span: a name, a start time and an end time.

41
00:02:06.241 --> 00:02:08.363
Like a scan in, and a scan out.

42
00:02:08.567 --> 00:02:10.419
The database write is short.

43
00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:12.007
The fraud check is long.

44
00:02:12.007 --> 00:02:14.389
And the card partner sits inside it.

45
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.824
Drawn like this, one row per span, the picture is called a waterfall.

46
00:02:19.824 --> 00:02:21.432
So, which step is slow?

47
00:02:21.432 --> 00:02:22.551
Pause and guess.

48
00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:28.819
The obvious answer is the longest bar, the API.

49
00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:32.325
Let's be fair to it: it really did take three seconds.

50
00:02:32.325 --> 00:02:33.819
But look underneath it.

51
00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:37.975
Almost all that time, the API was just waiting for its children.

52
00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.775
The fraud check, then?

53
00:02:39.775 --> 00:02:40.563
Same story.

54
00:02:40.563 --> 00:02:44.215
It is long, but it is waiting for the card partner.

55
00:02:44.433 --> 00:02:48.695
The slow step is the longest bar with nothing underneath it.

56
00:02:48.695 --> 00:02:51.892
Here, the card partner: 2.6 of the 3 seconds.

57
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.558
Waiting isn't working.

58
00:02:53.558 --> 00:02:57.468
A long bar can simply mean: I was waiting for someone else.

59
00:02:58.100 --> 00:02:59.061
Now the names.

60
00:02:59.061 --> 00:03:02.561
Every span in this trace carries the same trace ID.

61
00:03:02.561 --> 00:03:04.413
That's our tracking number.

62
00:03:04.633 --> 00:03:08.979
Each span also has its own span ID, and the ID of its parent.

63
00:03:08.979 --> 00:03:10.831
The parents make the tree.

64
00:03:10.831 --> 00:03:13.752
The first span, the API's, has no parent.

65
00:03:13.752 --> 00:03:15.177
It is the root span.

66
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.217
But these spans are made in different services.

67
00:03:19.217 --> 00:03:22.445
How does the card partner know which trace it is in?

68
00:03:22.445 --> 00:03:29.397
Each caller copies the number onto its outgoing request, in a header, an extra line sent with every web request.

69
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.178
The standard one is called traceparent.

70
00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:33.368
It has four parts.

71
00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:34.326
A version.

72
00:03:34.326 --> 00:03:37.593
The trace ID, the same for the whole request.

73
00:03:37.593 --> 00:03:40.932
The parent span ID, the caller's current span.

74
00:03:40.932 --> 00:03:44.997
And a flag: 01 means the caller is recording this trace.

75
00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.202
The caller injects the header.

76
00:03:47.202 --> 00:03:51.273
The receiver extracts it, and starts its own span as a child.

77
00:03:51.273 --> 00:03:55.877
Hop by hop, the trace ID stays the same, and only the parent changes.

78
00:03:55.877 --> 00:03:58.880
That hand-over is called context propagation.

79
00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:00.674
Now let's break it.

80
00:04:00.674 --> 00:04:06.357
Put a proxy, a program that forwards requests, between the fraud check and the card partner.

81
00:04:06.357 --> 00:04:09.013
Some proxies strip headers they don't know.

82
00:04:09.233 --> 00:04:10.608
The header goes in.

83
00:04:10.608 --> 00:04:12.127
It does not come out.

84
00:04:12.333 --> 00:04:16.797
So the card partner sees no tracking number, and starts a brand new one.

85
00:04:16.797 --> 00:04:18.533
A parcel with a fresh label.

86
00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:21.551
Now you have two traces that look unrelated.

87
00:04:21.551 --> 00:04:26.360
And in the first one, the fraud check is a long bar with nothing underneath.

88
00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.259
It looks guilty, and it isn't.

89
00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:32.790
So the point really is: a trace is only as long as its chain of headers.

90
00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:36.032
When it stops early, look for the hop that dropped it.

91
00:04:36.833 --> 00:04:38.285
Back to our question.

92
00:04:38.285 --> 00:04:42.984
hello-devops keeps its visit count in Redis, a small, fast database.

93
00:04:42.984 --> 00:04:48.651
In the lab, Redis writes were paused for 800 milliseconds, and one visit was sent.

94
00:04:48.867 --> 00:04:50.021
No code changed.

95
00:04:50.021 --> 00:04:56.656
Starting the app under a wrapper command, opentelemetry-instrument, was enough to get spans.

96
00:04:56.656 --> 00:05:00.407
The trace showed GET slash, taking 835 milliseconds.

97
00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:08.241
And under it, its child span INCRBY, the Redis command that adds to the count: 832 of those milliseconds.

98
00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:11.356
The longest bar with nothing underneath it.

99
00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:15.326
Status 200, zero errors, and still a slow Redis call.

100
00:05:15.326 --> 00:05:17.596
Now you know where to look next.

101
00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.663
A trace doesn't replace the others.

102
00:05:20.663 --> 00:05:23.184
A metric alert says something is wrong.

103
00:05:23.184 --> 00:05:24.477
A trace shows where.

104
00:05:24.477 --> 00:05:27.387
And the logs of that one request explain why.

105
00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.713
And recording every request costs CPU, network and storage.

106
00:05:31.713 --> 00:05:35.059
So at high traffic, teams keep only some traces.

107
00:05:35.059 --> 00:05:36.175
That's sampling.

108
00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:42.060
Head sampling decides at the start, and the 01 flag carries that choice to every service.

109
00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:46.830
It's cheap, but blind: it can't know yet which requests will fail or crawl.

110
00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:53.380
Tail sampling waits for the whole trace, keeps the errors and the slow ones, and pays for it in memory.

111
00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.244
So, the whole story in two lines.

112
00:05:56.244 --> 00:06:00.581
One tracking number, copied from hop to hop in the traceparent header.

113
00:06:00.581 --> 00:06:04.545
And the slow step is the longest bar with nothing underneath it.

114
00:06:04.767 --> 00:06:05.758
A quick check.

115
00:06:05.758 --> 00:06:10.855
A request crosses four services, but its trace shows only the first two.

116
00:06:10.855 --> 00:06:12.130
Where do you look?

117
00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:13.263
Pause and think.

118
00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:19.038
At the hop between the second and the third.

119
00:06:19.038 --> 00:06:23.712
Something there, a proxy, a queue, a homemade client, didn't pass the header on.

120
00:06:23.933 --> 00:06:27.964
Next, a new part about the calls between services themselves.

121
00:06:27.964 --> 00:06:32.523
When a call is slow or fails, who decides to wait, retry, or give up?
